<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Huexotzinco</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1521 CE (approx.) · capital Huexotzinco</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Nahua altepetl on the eastern slopes of Iztaccíhuatl that remained independent of the Aztecs and fought them in ritual 'flower wars'.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Huexotzinco (modern Huejotzingo) was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimec</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> groups in the Puebla valley in the 12th or 13th century, and grew into a powerful city-state that at times dominated neighbours such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cholula">Cholula</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>It was governed by a ruler (tlatoani) and nobles over a farming population in a well-watered valley. With <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cholula">Cholula</a> it formed an independent bloc east of the volcanoes, waging the ritualised xochiyaoyotl (flower wars) with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, in which warriors sought captives for sacrifice. In the early 16th century it suffered defeats by Tlaxcala and sought Aztec help.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Huexotzinco joined the Spanish against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> in 1519-21. Its nobles later sent a famous 1560 letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II</a> protesting excessive tribute. Its Franciscan monastery is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1200 CE – 1521 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.58%;width:6.39%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1521 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Huexotzinco</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesoamerican religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nahuatl</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Huejotzingo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1360&amp;focus=huexotzinco" aria-label="Huexotzinco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2019 1468 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Huexotzinco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2339 1724L2321 1717"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2339 1724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2339 1724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1360&amp;focus=huexotzinco">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
